---
import Frame from '../../components/PageFrame.astro';
const items = [
  {
    title: 'Stages and ownership',
    body: 'Every stage has a responsible role, a clear condition and an understandable next action.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Explicit approval gate',
    body: 'The example pauses for your decision. A suggestion cannot silently become an approved hand-off.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Complete activity history',
    body: 'Read the local sequence as you progress; reset it whenever you want to explore again.',
  },
];
---

<Frame label="Workflows" items={items}>
  <section class="container section demo-section" id="demo-guide">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">TRY IT / LOCAL DEMONSTRATION</p>
      <h2>A considered start, from brief to hand-off.</h2>
      <p>
        Advance the sample client-onboarding request through four stages. At
        Human review, inspect the proposed hand-off and explicitly approve it
        before completion. No business action or message is sent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container section split-section">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">DECISIONS WITH CONTEXT</p>
      <h2>The pause is part of the process.</h2>
      <p>
        An approval is useful when it gives a person enough context to make a
        decision. Our example holds the delivery step until you actively accept
        the proposed next action.
      </p>
      <p>
        For an actual team, define who may approve, what they should check and
        what happens when information is missing. A visually complete process
        can still be incomplete in practice.
      </p>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="/journal/mapping-a-repeatable-business-process/"
      >
        Map a repeatable process →
      </a>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Patterns worth making explicit</h3>
      <ol class="lifecycle">
        <li>
          <span class="number">A</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Project intake</h3>
            <p>
              Confirm the request, scope and first milestone before committing
              to delivery.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">B</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Document review</h3>
            <p>
              Route a proposed revision to the right reviewer and record the
              accepted decision.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">C</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Weekly coordination</h3>
            <p>
              Identify a blocker, give it an owner and agree on a specific next
              step.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container section reading">
    <h2>What does this example prove?</h2>
    <p>
      It demonstrates a deterministic frontend state model, visible ownership,
      keyboard controls and an inspectable activity history. It does not prove
      integration with your existing tools or savings from automation.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a source that explains each role, explore{' '}
      <a href="/products/knowledge/">Knowledge</a>. To inspect a separate
      fictional reporting dataset, try{' '}
      <a href="/products/insights/">Insights</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Frame>
